grammar Ll1FollowRestriction

// A's alternatives lead with A_ ("a") and AA ("aa"), and "a" is a prefix of "aa".
// The `!>> A_` on the first alternative resolves the conflict: on input `aa` the
// first alternative cannot match, because "a" is immediately followed by "a", so
// only the second alternative (AA) applies. The parse is unambiguous. The current
// prefix check ignores `!>>`, so A parses through GLL; a restriction-aware check
// could keep it LL(1) with the same result, since the restriction forbids exactly
// the character that extends "a" into "aa".
S = A
A = A_ !>> A_ | AA

@Regex
A_ = "a"

@Regex
AA = "aa"
